{"cuisine/cuisine-of-uttar-pradesh":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-uttar-pradesh","n":"Uttar Pradesh cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Cuisine of Uttar Pradesh is an umbrella term for various regional cuisines within the state of Uttar Pradesh (UP) located in Northern India.  As the state of Uttar Pradesh's borders were drawn by the British and in disregard to native regions, \"Uttar Pradeshi\" cuisine refers to many different cultural regions with their own unique cuisines. They are Brajwasi, Mughlai, Awadhi, Kannuaji, Kauravi, Bundeli, Bagheli and Bhojpuri. The cuisines of UP are very diverse, and consist of both vegetarian and non-vegetarian dishes, with the Brajwasi cuisine being more vegetarian like its Rajasthani and Haryanvi neighbors. Being a large state, the various cuisines of UP share many dishes and recipes with the neighboring states of Delhi, Rajasthan, Uttarakhand, Bihar, Jharkhand and Haryana."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-uttar-pradesh"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Uttar_Pradesh"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","ref":"site/archanaskitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Uttar Pradesh section of Archana Doshi's Indian recipe site.","url":"https://www.archanaskitchen.com/cuisine/uttar-pradesh","suggested":true},{"name":"Dassana's Veg Recipes (Punjabi)","ref":"site/vegrecipesofindia-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vegrecipesofindia.com","note":"Uttar Pradesh section of Dassana Amit's Indian vegetarian recipe blog.","url":"https://www.vegrecipesofindia.com/recipes/uttar-pradesh","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Uttar Pradesh cooking","count":1,"links":[["Awadhi cuisine","cuisine/awadhi-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/oriya-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/oriya-cuisine","n":"Oriya cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["East Indian cuisine","cuisine/east-india-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["The cuisine of Odisha is the cuisine of the Indian state of Odisha. Compared to other regional Indian cuisines, Odia cuisine uses less oil and is less spicy, while nonetheless remaining flavorful. Rice is the staple food of this region. Mustard oil is used in some dishes as the cooking medium, but ghee (made of cow's milk) is preferred in temples. Odia foods are traditionally served either on brass or bronze metal plates, banana leaves, or disposable plates made of sal leaves."],["Odia cooks, particularly from the Puri region, were much sought after due to their ability to cook food in accordance with the Hindu scriptures."],["Yoghurt is used in many Odia dishes. Many sweets of the region are based on chhena (cheese)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"oriya-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oriya_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Away in the Kitchen (Odia home cooking)","ref":"site/awayinthekitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"awayinthekitchen.com","note":"Blog section devoted to Odia home recipes.","url":"https://www.awayinthekitchen.com/odia-home-cooking","suggested":true},{"name":"Archana's Kitchen (Oriya)","ref":"site/archanaskitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"archanaskitchen.com","note":"Odia cuisine section of Archana Doshi's large Indian recipe site.","url":"https://archanaskitchen.com/cuisine/oriya","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Odia Byanjana","ref":"cookbook/odia-byanjana","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Debasish Patanaik","note":"Survey of Odisha's cooking traditions with recipes, published by Aleph.","url":"https://www.alephbookcompany.com/book/odia-byanjana/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Dahibara Aloodum","dish/dahibara-aloodum"],["Dalma (dish)","dish/dalma-dish"],["Red ant chutney","dish/red-ant-chutney"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Dahi (curd)","ingredient/dahi-curd"],["Dahi vada","ingredient/dahi-vada"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Vada","ingredient/vada"],["Oecophylla smaragdina","ingredient/oecophylla-smaragdina"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["East Indian cuisine","cuisine/east-india-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/bc/Traditional_Odial_Lunch_Thali.jpg/500px-Traditional_Odial_Lunch_Thali.jpg","w":500,"h":392,"by":"Swetapadma07","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traditional_Odial_Lunch_Thali.jpg"}},"cuisine/icelandic-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/icelandic-cuisine","n":"Icelandic cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Iceland","intro":[["The cuisine of Iceland has a long history. Important parts of Icelandic cuisine are lamb, dairy, and fish, the latter because Iceland has traditionally been inhabited only near its coastline. Common foods in Iceland include skyr, hangikjöt (smoked lamb), kleinur, laufabrauð, and bollur. Þorramatur is a traditional buffet served at midwinter festivals called Þorrablót; it includes a selection of traditionally cured meat and fish products served with rúgbrauð (dense dark and sweet rye bread) and brennivín (an Icelandic akvavit). The flavors of this traditional country food originate in its preservation methods: pickling in fermented whey or brine, drying, and smoking."],["Modern Icelandic chefs usually emphasise the quality of available ingredients rather than age-old cooking traditions and methods. Numerous restaurants in Iceland specialise in seafood. At the annual Food and Fun chef's competition (held since 2004), competitors create innovative dishes with fresh ingredients produced in Iceland. Points of pride are the quality of the lamb meat, seafood, and, more recently, skyr. Other local ingredients include seabirds and waterfowl (including their eggs), salmon and trout, crowberry, blueberry, rhubarb, Iceland moss, wild mushrooms, wild thyme, lovage, angelica, and dried seaweed, as well as a wide array of dairy products."],["Because of the history of settlement in a harsh climate, animal products dominate Icelandic cuisine. Popular taste has been developing, however, to become closer to the European norm. As an example, consumption of vegetables has greatly increased in recent decades while consumption of fish has diminished, yet is still far higher than any other developed country at about quadruple the average."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"icelandic-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Icelandic_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Icelandic Food and Cookery","ref":"cookbook/icelandic-food-and-cookery","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Nanna Rögnvaldardóttir","note":"Introduction to traditional Icelandic dishes by Iceland's best-known food writer.","url":"https://forlagid.is/vara/icelandic-food-and-cookery","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Hákarl","dish/hakarl"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Greenland shark","ingredient/greenland-shark"],["Shark meat","ingredient/shark-meat"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Rye bread","ingredient/rye-bread"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/42/Hakarl_near_Bjarnah%C3%B6fn_in_Iceland.JPG/500px-Hakarl_near_Bjarnah%C3%B6fn_in_Iceland.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Chris 73 / Wikimedia Commons","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hakarl_near_Bjarnah%C3%B6fn_in_Iceland.JPG"}},"cuisine/montenegrin-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/montenegrin-cuisine","n":"Montenegrin cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Montenegro","intro":[["Montenegrin cuisine is a result of Montenegro's geographic position and its long history and tradition."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"montenegrin-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montenegrin_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Balkan Lunchbox: Kosovo recipes","ref":"site/balkanlunchbox-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"balkanlunchbox.com","note":"Balkan home cooking blog with a section of Montenegrin dishes.","url":"https://balkanlunchbox.com/category/country/montenegro-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Balkan cuisine","cuisine/balkan-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/43/%C4%86evap%C4%8Di%C4%87i_in_Montenegro.jpg/500px-%C4%86evap%C4%8Di%C4%87i_in_Montenegro.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Spacekid","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%86evap%C4%8Di%C4%87i_in_Montenegro.jpg"}},"cuisine/metis-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/metis-foodways","n":"Métis foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Prairie provinces, Canada","names":"Métis, Michif","intro":[["Métis food grew out of the buffalo hunt and the fur trade on the Prairies. ",["Bison","ingredient/bison"]," was the main meat, dried into jerky or pounded and mixed with fat and berries as ",["Pemmican","dish/pemmican"],", the food of voyageurs and winter travelers. ",["Rubaboo","dish/rubaboo"]," is a stew of peas or corn thickened with pemmican, and boulettes, meatballs of moose or elk, simmer in broth."],[["Bannock","dish/bannock-indigenous-american-food"],", or galette, a flat bread of flour, water and fat, is baked on a griddle, on a stick or in a pan by the fire, and fried as beignes. Chokecherries, saskatoons, blueberries and cranberries are picked through summer, fish like ",["Whitefish","ingredient/whitefish"]," and goldeye are smoked, and ",["Wild rice","ingredient/wild-rice"]," goes into pancakes."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Gabriel Dumont Institute, Virtual Museum of Métis History and Culture, Traditional Métis foods","url":"https://www.metismuseum.ca/media/document.php/14522.Traditional Metis Foods revised May 2018.pdf"},{"name":"Gabriel Dumont Institute, Virtual Museum of Métis History and Culture, Traditional Métis food","url":"https://www.metismuseum.ca/media/document.php/00746.Traditional Métis Food.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"metis-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Bannock (Indigenous American food)","dish/bannock-indigenous-american-food"],["Pemmican","dish/pemmican"],["Rubaboo","dish/rubaboo"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Bison","ingredient/bison"],["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Berry","ingredient/berry"],["Pea","ingredient/pea"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/51/Inuit_bannock.JPG/500px-Inuit_bannock.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"CambridgeBayWeather","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inuit_bannock.JPG","of":"Bannock (Indigenous American food)"}},"ingredient/gochujang":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/gochujang","n":"Gochujang","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 4 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Gochujang or red chili paste is a savory, sweet, and spicy fermented  condiment popular in Korean cooking. It is made from gochugaru  (red chili powder), chapssal (glutinous rice), meju (fermented soybean) powder, yeotgireum (barley malt powder), and salt. The sweetness comes from the starch of cooked glutinous rice, cultured with saccharifying enzymes during the fermentation process. Traditionally, it would be naturally fermented over years in jangdok (earthenware) on an elevated stone platform called jangdokdae in the backyard."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-gochujang"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gochujang"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine",[["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Tteokbokki","dish/tteokbokki"]]],["South Korean cuisine","cuisine/south-korean-cuisine",[["Jjolmyeon","dish/jjolmyeon"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Namul","ingredient/namul"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Corn starch","ingredient/corn-starch"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Noodle","ingredient/noodle"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Alaska pollock","ingredient/alaska-pollock"],["Chili powder","ingredient/chili-powder"],["Fishcake","ingredient/fishcake"],["Tteok","ingredient/tteok"]]}]},"ingredient/crab-meat":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/crab-meat","n":"Crab meat","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Crab meat, also known as crab marrow, is the edible meat found in a crab, specifically in its legs and claws. It is widely used in global cuisines for its soft, delicate and sweet flavor."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-crab-meat"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crab_meat"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"]]],["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"]]],["Western American cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-the-western-united-states",[["Crab Louie","dish/crab-louie"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Crayfish as food","ingredient/crayfish-as-food"],["Crustacean","ingredient/crustacean"],["Langoustine","ingredient/langoustine"],["Lobster meat","ingredient/lobster-meat"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["White wine","ingredient/white-wine"],["Asparagus","ingredient/asparagus"],["Hard-boiled egg","ingredient/hard-boiled-egg"],["Iceberg lettuce","ingredient/iceberg-lettuce"],["Louis dressing","ingredient/louis-dressing"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/f9/Ristet_br%C3%B8d_med_krabbekl%C3%B8er_%284898535215%29.jpg/500px-Ristet_br%C3%B8d_med_krabbekl%C3%B8er_%284898535215%29.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"cyclonebill from Copenhagen, Denmark","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ristet_br%C3%B8d_med_krabbekl%C3%B8er_(4898535215).jpg"}},"dish/blooming-onion":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/blooming-onion","n":"Blooming onion","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["A blooming onion, also called onion bloom, onion blossom, onion flower, bloomin' onion, or onion mum, is a dish consisting of one large onion, cut to resemble a flower (after it has expanded while soaking in ice water), battered, and deep-fried, often served with dipping sauce. It is served as an appetizer at some restaurants."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-blooming-onion"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blooming_onion"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Batter (cooking)","ingredient/batter-cooking"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["5-8 Club","dish/5-8-club"],["Altabira City Tavern","dish/altabira-city-tavern"],["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Applebee's","dish/applebee-s"],["Arby's","dish/arby-s"],["Arnold's Bar and Grill","dish/arnold-s-bar-and-grill"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Be Our Guest Restaurant","dish/be-our-guest-restaurant"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Ben's Chili Bowl","dish/ben-s-chili-bowl"],["Benjy's","dish/benjy-s"],["Besaw's","dish/besaw-s"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blackhawk (restaurant)","dish/blackhawk-restaurant"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Bobcat Bite","dish/bobcat-bite"],["Boiling Springs Tavern","dish/boiling-springs-tavern"],["Brix Tavern","dish/brix-tavern"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brown Derby","dish/brown-derby"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Buona","dish/buona"],["Busboys and Poets","dish/busboys-and-poets"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Byways Cafe","dish/byways-cafe"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["California Grill","dish/california-grill"],["Camp Washington Chili","dish/camp-washington-chili"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Carnation Cafe","dish/carnation-cafe"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chiltern Firehouse","dish/chiltern-firehouse"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d0/Blooming_onion.jpg/500px-Blooming_onion.jpg","w":500,"h":374,"by":"No machine-readable author provided. Waptaff assumed (based on copyright claims).","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blooming_onion.jpg"}},"dish/butter-mochi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/butter-mochi","n":"Butter mochi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hawaiian cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Butter mochi is a cake made from coconut milk, glutinous rice flour (mochiko), sugar, butter, and eggs. It is a popular dessert in Hawaiian cuisine, where it is more popular than brownies are in the continental US. Having originated in Hawaii, it is an example of Hawaiian local food. It has been described as \"a combo of cake and mochi.\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-butter-mochi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butter_mochi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hawaiian cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-hawaii"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Mochiko","ingredient/mochiko"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Onolicious Hawaii","ref":"site/onolicioushawaii-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"onolicioushawaii.com","note":"Hawaii-based blog of local-style recipes.","url":"https://onolicioushawaii.com","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Hawaiian cuisine","links":[["Ate-Oh-Ate","dish/ate-oh-ate"],["Evil jungle prince","dish/evil-jungle-prince"],["Hula's Island Grill","dish/hula-s-island-grill"],["Poi (Hawaiian food)","dish/poi-hawaiian-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/cb/Slices_of_butter_mochi.jpg/500px-Slices_of_butter_mochi.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"RudolfSchreier","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slices_of_butter_mochi.jpg"}},"dish/cha-chaan-teng":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cha-chaan-teng","n":"Cha chaan teng","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hong Kong cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Cha chaan teng, often called a Hong Kong-style cafe or diner in English, is a type of restaurant that originated in Hong Kong. Cha chaan tengs are commonly found in Hong Kong, Macau, and parts of Guangdong. Due to the waves of mass migrations from Hong Kong in the 1980s, they are now established in major Chinese communities in Western countries such as Australia, Canada, the United Kingdom, and the United States.  Likened to a greasy spoon cafe or an American diner, cha chaan tengs are known for eclectic and affordable menus, which include dishes from Hong Kong cuisine and Hong Kong-style Western cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cha-chaan-teng"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cha_chaan_teng"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hong Kong cuisine","cuisine/hong-kong-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Hong Kong cuisine","links":[["Baked pork chop rice","dish/baked-pork-chop-rice"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Tim Ho Wan","dish/tim-ho-wan"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b7/Cha_Chaan_Teng_7267.JPG/500px-Cha_Chaan_Teng_7267.JPG","w":500,"h":281,"by":"T1NH0 (talk)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cha_Chaan_Teng_7267.JPG"}},"ingredient/ipomoea-aquatica":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ipomoea-aquatica","n":"Ipomoea aquatica","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 5 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Ipomoea aquatica, commonly known as water spinach or kangkung,  is a semi-aquatic, tropical plant grown as a vegetable for its tender shoots. I. aquatica is generally believed to have been first domesticated in Southeast Asia. It is widely cultivated in Southeast Asia, East Asia, and South Asia. It grows abundantly near waterways and requires little to no care."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ipomoea-aquatica"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ipomoea_aquatica"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Kuluban","dish/kuluban"],["Lotek (food)","dish/lotek-food"],["Plecing kangkung","dish/plecing-kangkung"],["Tinutuan","dish/tinutuan"]]],["Balinese cuisine","cuisine/balinese-cuisine",[["Plecing kangkung","dish/plecing-kangkung"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Ghost pepper","ingredient/ghost-pepper"],["Krupuk","ingredient/krupuk"],["Lontong","ingredient/lontong"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Rice cake","ingredient/rice-cake"],["Seedling","ingredient/seedling"],["Tempeh","ingredient/tempeh"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/10/N_Ipoa_D1600.JPG/500px-N_Ipoa_D1600.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Eric Guinther","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N_Ipoa_D1600.JPG"}},"dish/jinya-ramen-bar":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/jinya-ramen-bar","n":"Jinya Ramen Bar","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","d